<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Guam</h1><p class="st-meta">1898 CE – present · capital Hagatna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest island of the Marianas, a Spanish colony for two centuries and an American territory since 1898, now a key US military base in the Pacific.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chamorro-people">Chamorro people</a> settled Guam some 3,500 years ago. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Magellan</a> reached it in 1521, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> colonised it from 1668, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-luis-de-san-vitores">Diego Luis de San Vitores</a> founded a mission; wars and disease drastically reduced the Chamorro. In 1898, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish-American War</a>, the cruiser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uss-charleston-c-2">USS Charleston</a> captured the island, and Spain ceded it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">US Navy</a> governed Guam as a naval station, while the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Marianas</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Japan occupied Guam from December 1941 until American forces retook it in July 1944, an ordeal still commemorated on Liberation Day. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guam-organic-act-of-1950">Organic Act</a> of 1950 made Guamanians US citizens and created a civilian government, though they cannot vote for president.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Guam elected its first governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-camacho">Carlos Camacho</a>, in 1970. It remains an unincorporated territory of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>, dependent on military bases such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andersen-air-force-base">Andersen Air Force Base</a> and on tourism, with continuing debate over its political status.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/guam.jpg" alt="Guam" width="320" height="173"><figcaption>Image: Xrmap · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Guam.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1898 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hagatna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Carlos Camacho</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Chamorro</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guahan, Territory of Guam</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1962&amp;focus=guam" aria-label="Guam on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8648 1675 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Guam lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8968 1931L8309 1890M8968 1931L2570 1008M8968 1931L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8309 1890h0M2570 1008h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8968 1931h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8968 1931h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hagatna. <a href="/atlas?year=1962&amp;focus=guam">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
